==English==
{{wikipedia|Cloud Nine|lang=en}}
===Etymology===
* Popular etymology references the 1896 edition of the International Cloud Atlas which defined ten types of cloud. The book defined the ninth cloud as the [[cumulonimbus]], which rises to 10 km (6.2 miles), the highest a cloud can be. No conclusive evidence, however, confirms this origin.
* The 1960 print of the Dictionary of American Slang (Wentworth & Flexner) includes “cloud seven”, which seems to predate “cloud nine” by a few years.  Other etymologies reference Buddhist or Christian lore, and even Dante’s Divine Comedy, but again, no evidence prefers these versions.
* In Chinese, glyph elements of "quick movements, erect" with "forward thrusting, kneeling forward" suggests the NINE required kowtows when appearing before the emperor of China: kneeling or lying prostrate and while down on the floor, touching your forehead to the floor three times, returning to an erect standing position, then kneeling and kowtowing again, etc., also performed three times, known as "three kneelings and NINE kowtows"; when used as an element and not as a stand-alone glyph, "quick movements" with "forward thrusting" depicts SPURTING, such as might occur after hacking someone with a sword; in Chinese, the moment of the "clouds and the rain" refers to orgasm, with the glyph meaning "cloud" also as a "loose and dripping short arm," and "rain" as "sticky fluids flowing down from spread legs" ...and CLOUD NINE is another expression brought back to England in the early 18th century by the expelled opium drug pushers, the moment of male orgasm, combining the two glyphs "cloud" and "nine" or "short arm loose and dripping after spurting"; the numerals one through ten are all associated with hand gestures, used in China to avoid confusion while bargaining or simply due to pronunciation problems using different dialects, or while bidding at an auction and keeping the bid quiet; the gesture for NINE was to shape the index finger into a hook while touching the tip of the thumb to the middle finger, not unlike the shape of the Arabic numeral for NINE; this same gesture is used in many parts of the world as a rude gesture representing a male spurting due to self-stimulation, and often has this same connotation within glyphs

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[cloud]] [[nine]]|-}}

# {{idiomatic}} A state of [[happiness]], [[elation]] or [[bliss]]; often used in the phrase [[on cloud nine]]
#: ''He was on '''cloud nine''' for days after she agreed to marry him.''

